SINA_SMART["pharma-dynamics"] = {"lecture":"pharma-dynamics","title":"Pharmacodynamics","mapStatus":"auto: generated by universal.py from the lecture summary and key points; not human-reviewed","los":[{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e0","text":"Pharmacodynamics: what the drug does to the body; pharmacokinetics: what the body does to the drug.","topic":"The big picture","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e1","text":"Most drugs bind reversibly (hydrogen, ionic, van der Waals); covalent = irreversible.","topic":"Drugs and receptors","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e2","text":"Chirality matters: (S)-carvedilol is the potent β-blocker.","topic":"Drugs and receptors","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e10","text":"Benzodiazepines: allosteric modulators of GABA-A.","topic":"Types of ligands","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e6","text":"Partial agonists: lower maximum (buprenorphine, pentazocine).","topic":"Types of ligands","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e7","text":"Inverse agonist reduces constitutive activity (nalmefene).","topic":"Types of ligands","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e3","text":"Lower Kd = higher affinity.","topic":"Dose-response relationships","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e4","text":"Potency = EC₅₀/ED₅₀; efficacy = Emax.","topic":"Dose-response relationships","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e5","text":"Fentanyl ≈ 100× more potent than morphine.","topic":"Dose-response relationships","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e8","text":"Competitive antagonist: curve right, efficacy unchanged (naloxone, propranolol).","topic":"Dose-response relationships","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e9","text":"Irreversible antagonist: lower Emax (phenoxybenzamine).","topic":"Dose-response relationships","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:s4","text":"Describe and explain: Receptor families","topic":"Receptor families","sec":4,"importance":"standard","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e11","text":"Therapeutic index = TD₅₀ / ED₅₀; narrow for lithium, digoxin, phenytoin, theophylline.","topic":"Quantal responses and safety","sec":5,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"pharma-dynamics:e12","text":"Tolerance develops slowly; tachyphylaxis rapidly.","topic":"Quantal responses and safety","sec":5,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"}],"vmap":{},"mlevel":{"1dn277i":"a","gahida":"a","wkccq8":"a","12fo5wz":"a","wd32o8":"a","1xk35sq":"a","1s4cps4":"a","1v4oh8i":"a","1m8ds5z":"a","ffbyl":"a","25r7wi":"a","sp3ops":"a","4n5997":"a","w56uh9":"a","1gtnmk2":"a","zy6s42":"a","g2izyi":"a","1jpegh8":"a","15isjh0":"a","tr057j":"a","14pi51o":"x","9yrtgt":"x"},"mapCounts":{"verified":0,"auto":20,"ambiguous":0,"missing":2},"mrules":[],"items":[],"experimental":[],"status":{},"difficulty":{},"expl":{"pharma-dynamics:e0":{"s":0,"u":["Pharmacodynamics: what the drug does to the body; pharmacokinetics: what the body does to the drug.","Two divisions: pharmacodynamics (what the drug does to the body: potency, efficacy, dose-response, mechanism) and pharmacokinetics (what the body does to the drug)."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e1":{"s":1,"u":["Most drugs bind reversibly (hydrogen, ionic, van der Waals); covalent = irreversible.","Bonds: covalent (strongest, usually irreversible), electrostatic, hydrophobic; binding is usually reversible (hydrogen, ionic, van der Waals)."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e2":{"s":1,"u":["Chirality matters: (S)-carvedilol is the potent β-blocker.","Shape matters like a key in a lock, and so does chirality : the (S)(-) isomer of carvedilol is a potent β-blocker, the (R)(+) isomer 100-fold weaker at β but equal at α."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e10":{"s":2,"u":["Benzodiazepines: allosteric modulators of GABA-A.","Allosteric modulators bind elsewhere and enhance or inhibit (benzodiazepines enhance GABA at the GABA-A chloride channel); allosteric inhibition is not overcome by more agonist."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e6":{"s":2,"u":["Partial agonists: lower maximum (buprenorphine, pentazocine).","Partial agonists give a lower maximum whatever the dose (pentazocine; buprenorphine is safer than morphine because it causes less respiratory depression)."],"dp":[{"h":"Why buprenorphine is safer than morphine","t":"As a partial agonist, buprenorphine can only activate µ-opioid receptors part of the way, even at full occupancy. Its respiratory depression therefore plateaus, while a full agonist's keeps rising with dose. It also occupies receptors tightly, blunting additional opioids taken on top. Source: the lecture's partial agonist slide."}]},"pharma-dynamics:e7":{"s":2,"u":["Inverse agonist reduces constitutive activity (nalmefene).","Inverse agonists prefer Rᵢ and reduce basal activity (nalmefene).","The receptor exists in inactive (Rᵢ) and active (Rₐ) states with some constitutive activity."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e3":{"s":3,"u":["Lower Kd = higher affinity.","Kd is the concentration occupying 50% of receptors: the lower the Kd, the higher the affinity .","Affinity : attraction to the receptor."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e4":{"s":3,"u":["Potency = EC₅₀/ED₅₀; efficacy = Emax.","Efficacy : maximal effect ( Emax ); clinically efficacy usually matters more (loop diuretics outperform others).","Potency : dose needed for 50% of the maximal effect ( EC₅₀ / ED₅₀ ); fentanyl is about 100 times more potent than morphine."],"dp":[{"h":"Why efficacy usually matters more than potency","t":"A more potent drug simply needs a smaller dose; as long as that dose can be given safely, potency changes little for the patient. Efficacy sets the ceiling: if a drug cannot produce a strong enough effect, no dose will help. That is why a loop diuretic that removes more fluid is preferred in severe oedema, whatever its milligram dose. Source: the lecture's potency vs efficacy slides."}]},"pharma-dynamics:e5":{"s":3,"u":["Fentanyl ≈ 100× more potent than morphine.","Potency : dose needed for 50% of the maximal effect ( EC₅₀ / ED₅₀ ); fentanyl is about 100 times more potent than morphine."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e8":{"s":3,"u":["Competitive antagonist: curve right, efficacy unchanged (naloxone, propranolol).","Competitive antagonist (phentolamine, naloxone, propranolol): same site, reversible, curve shifts right, potency falls, efficacy unchanged , overcome by more agonist; its effect varies with plasma level and with agonist level (exercise can overcome propranolol).","Spare receptors : a maximal response can occur without full occupancy; a little antagonist only shifts the curve, more antagonist lowers the maximum."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e9":{"s":3,"u":["Irreversible antagonist: lower Emax (phenoxybenzamine).","Irreversible/non-competitive antagonist ( phenoxybenzamine , used in phaeochromocytoma): lowers maximal efficacy ; duration depends on receptor turnover; overdose must be countered physiologically.","Spare receptors : a maximal response can occur without full occupancy; a little antagonist only shifts the curve, more antagonist lowers the maximum."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:s4":{"s":4,"u":["(1) Intracellular receptors for lipid-soluble ligands (corticosteroids, mineralocorticoids, sex steroids, vitamin D, thyroid hormone) that regulate gene transcription.","(2) Enzyme-linked receptors (insulin, EGF, PDGF, ANP, TGF-β) activating tyrosine kinase, serine kinase or guanylyl cyclase."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e11":{"s":5,"u":["Therapeutic index = TD₅₀ / ED₅₀; narrow for lithium, digoxin, phenytoin, theophylline.","Small TI (narrow window, under about 2): lithium, digoxin, phenytoin, theophylline, chemotherapy , which need titration and monitoring.","Therapeutic index = TD₅₀ / ED₅₀ : the larger it is, the wider the therapeutic window."],"dp":[]},"pharma-dynamics:e12":{"s":5,"u":["Tolerance develops slowly; tachyphylaxis rapidly.","Variable responses : idiosyncratic, hypo- or hyper-reactive; tolerance (response falls with continued use) and tachyphylaxis (falls very rapidly)."],"dp":[]}},"meta":{"1dn277i":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e0/recall/1"},"gahida":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e1/recall/1"},"wkccq8":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e0/recall/2"},"12fo5wz":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e2/recall/1"},"wd32o8":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e3/recall/1"},"1xk35sq":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e4/recall/1"},"1s4cps4":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e4/recall/1"},"1v4oh8i":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e6/recall/1"},"1m8ds5z":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e7/recall/1"},"ffbyl":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e8/recall/1"},"25r7wi":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e9/recall/1"},"sp3ops":{"a":"relationship","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e8/relationship/1"},"4n5997":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e10/recall/1"},"w56uh9":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:s4/recall/1"},"1gtnmk2":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:s4/recall/2"},"zy6s42":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e11/recall/1"},"g2izyi":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e11/recall/1"},"1jpegh8":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e12/recall/1"},"15isjh0":{"a":"application","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e5/application/1"},"tr057j":{"a":"recall","vg":"pharma-dynamics:e10/recall/2"}},"explRev":"5ccb51b8e872"};
